Durarara!! AU
Chapter 18 – Part 2
Curtis’s hand stayed steady around hers, long black braids shifting in the roof wind as he gave a small nod.
“Of course, Celty.”
No argument. No push for more definition than she had been willing to give. Just acceptance, plain and immediate, as if the most important thing on the roof were not winning the shape of the future but not dropping the present now that she had finally admitted it was different.
Celty’s black smoke thinned into a slower, less defensive ribbon above her collar. For a second she only stood there with her gloved fingers closed around his, the city moving under them, the morning suddenly wider because neither of them was climbing back down into pretense.
When her real voice came through the private channel, it was quieter than it had been all morning.
“…Thank you.”
A short pause.
“Most people hear uncertainty and try to close it into something they can own. You just… left room in it.” The smoke drifted across their joined hands. “That may be the most dangerous thing about you.”
Another softer beat.
“Don’t let go yet.” Her grip tightened once, not dramatic, only decided. “If this morning is only honesty without a conclusion, then I want the honesty to include this: standing here with you is already better than the version where I put the helmet on and pretend I came up for air alone.”
They stayed on the roof longer than either of them had planned.
Curtis did not let go of her hand. Celty did not ask him to. The morning wind moved through his long black braids and tore thin ribbons from the black smoke above her collar, and for a while Ikebukuro was only background noise beneath a silence that no longer felt like avoidance. It felt like the first unchallenged pause either of them had managed since the night began.
Eventually Curtis tipped his head toward the stairwell.
“…Coffee’s going to be cold downstairs. I can make more.”
Celty’s real voice came through the private channel, quieter than the argumentative version that had chased him up here.
“That sounds ordinary enough to be suspicious.”
“Good,” he said. “Ordinary might be useful.”
They went back down together.

In the flat, the bedroom still carried the disorder of the morning — covers thrown, his phone face-down on the sheet, the yellow helmet waiting on the table like a reminder that the outside world had only been postponed. Curtis released her hand only long enough to start a fresh pot of coffee. Celty remained near the counter instead of reclaiming distance, black riding suit stark against the pale kitchen light, smoke rising in slower, calmer lines now that the roof had taken the edge off the fight.
Neither of them reopened the largest questions.
No list.
No aging.
No Rio.
No Shinra by name.
The restraint was deliberate. They had agreed the morning could be honesty without a conclusion, and for once both of them seemed willing to protect that smaller, more fragile version of the truth.
Curtis poured two mugs, then paused.
“…You don’t drink coffee.”
Celty’s smoke curled once.
“I can hold the cup and pretend. It makes the scene look more normal.”
He huffed a soft breath that was almost a laugh and passed it to her anyway. Their fingers brushed around the ceramic. Neither pretended it was accidental.

They ended up on the couch.
Not entangled. Not performing careful separation either. Curtis sat with one arm along the back cushion; Celty sat close enough that the outside of her thigh rested against his. The television stayed off. The city remained a muted presence beyond the windows. Every so often his phone buzzed with some harmless notification he did not check. Every so often her smoke drifted across his sleeve and then retreated like a thought she had decided not to voice yet.
After a stretch of quiet, Celty’s real voice returned, low and almost practical.
“If anyone asks where I was overnight… I was driving.”
Curtis glanced sideways, mismatched eyes half-lidded.
“And if anyone asks why I stayed in all day?”
“You can tell them the truth,” she said. “Classes were canceled. You were tired.” A short pause. “Both of those are accurate enough to survive inspection.”
He nodded once, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt.
“Celty.”
“Mm.”
“Thanks for not leaving when it got ugly.”
Black smoke rose in a softer ribbon above her collar. Her gloved hand found his again on the cushion between them, no ceremony in it, only continuity.
“…Don’t thank me like I did you a favor,” she answered. “I stayed because the alternative was putting the helmet on and being alone with the same thoughts in a colder room. That isn’t generosity. That’s preference.”
Curtis turned his palm upward under hers and let the contact settle.
Outside, the canceled school day continued without them.
Inside, for a few more hours at least, neither one reached for an emergency exit.
And that, for the moment, was the whole of the conclusion they were willing to afford.
Curtis looked at the untouched coffee in her gloved hand, then at the black smoke rising from the empty space above her collar, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as a faint, dry note entered his voice.
“I’m by no means a normal human myself. So don’t force yourself to pretend to be normal… or whatever normal is.”
The line landed quietly, but it cut cleanly through the small performance of the held mug and the carefully ordinary posture she had put on for the kitchen.
Celty was still for a second. Then the black smoke above her collar loosened, less controlled, less arranged for anyone else’s comfort. When her real voice came through the private channel, it carried a low, almost relieved roughness.
“…Good.”
A short pause.
“Because I’m not very good at the performance for long. I can hold a cup. I can sit on a couch. I can use the right words to keep other people from panicking about what I am.” The smoke drifted across his sleeve without apology. “But if you’re going to tell me not to bother, then I won’t waste the energy pretending this is a normal morning between normal people. It isn’t. You’re marked by things most humans don’t survive. I’m sitting here without a head. We already passed ordinary several conversations ago.”
Another quieter beat. Her gloved fingers tightened slightly around the mug she was never going to drink.
“It’s easier, hearing you say that. Most people want the reassuring version of me. The one that fits into a room without forcing them to revise what they believe is possible.” The empty line of her neck inclined toward him. “If you don’t need that version… then I’d rather stay as I am and let the morning be strange without apologizing for it.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes rested on the black smoke rising from the empty space above her collar, then on the pale line of her neck, the riding suit, the impossible fact of her simply sitting there with him. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as a small, unguarded smile touched his mouth.
“Your beauty never scared me, miss shadow woman.”
The private channel went quiet.
Black smoke above Celty’s collar stuttered, then rose in a thicker, uneven ribbon that gave her away more honestly than any expression could have. For a moment she only sat there with the untouched coffee cup in her gloved hands, as if the line had landed in a place she kept carefully defended for a reason.
When her real voice finally came through, it was lower and less steady than before.
“…You can’t keep saying things like that.”
A short pause.
“People stare at me and see an urban legend. A courier. A monster in a helmet. A problem to route around or a myth to chase.” The smoke drifted toward him despite the complaint. “Very few look at the shadow and the absence and still use the word beauty without flinching first. Fewer still say it like it costs them nothing.”
Another quieter beat.
“It doesn’t scare you. I know that. You’ve made that obvious since the first time you answered my actual voice.” Her gloved fingers tightened around the mug. “What unsettles me is how easily you say it. Like the most impossible parts of me are not obstacles to be tolerated… just details of someone you already decided to keep looking at.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him, softer than the protest.
“…Miss shadow woman. Ridiculous.” A thinner curl of smoke. “Say it again and I may start believing you mean it in the way that causes trouble.”
Curtis’s smile lingered, small and unhelpful, long black braids shifting as he watched the black smoke above her collar give her away.
“Yeah. And what kind of trouble is that?”
Celty went still. The smoke rose harder, then twisted as if it wanted to retreat and failed. For a second the private channel held only the strain of a woman realizing she had left herself an opening and he had walked straight into it.
When her real voice came through, it was quieter, more exposed, and carefully exact.
“…The kind where I stop treating this morning like a temporary exception.”
A short pause.
“The kind where I start expecting your voice. Where another girl’s name on your phone lands with weight it shouldn’t. Where I climb roofs over arguments that were never only arguments, and then sit on your couch holding coffee I can’t drink because the scene feels too much like something I could want again tomorrow.” The smoke drifted across his sleeve. “The kind of trouble where ‘friend’ remains true and still becomes insufficient as a container.”
Another heavier beat.
“I already have a life arranged around someone else’s idea of permanence. If I start believing you mean every soft, dangerous thing you say… then I have to admit I am no longer only visiting this room. I am becoming altered by it.” Her gloved fingers tightened around the mug. “That is the trouble, Curtis. Not fear of you. Fear of how easily I could start rearranging myself around the fact that you look at me and do not flinch.”
Curtis nodded once, the small smile fading into something more careful. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he looked away toward the window.
“I understand. Alright. I won’t say it anymore.”
The compliance should have felt like relief.
It didn’t.
Celty’s black smoke jerked upward from the empty space above her collar, then hung there unsettled, as if the answer had landed in the wrong shape. She had warned him. She had named the trouble. She had told him what it would cost if she started believing the soft things he said. And now that he had done the responsible version of listening — withdrawing the words, closing the door she herself had pointed at — some contrary part of her immediately wanted the danger back.
The private channel stayed silent a second too long.
When her real voice finally came through, it was quieter and less certain than the warning had been.
“…That isn’t—”
She stopped. Started again.
“I mean. Yes. That is the sensible response.” A thin ribbon of smoke drifted toward him, reluctant and betraying. “You asked what kind of trouble. I told you. You respected the boundary in the answer. That should be the end of it.”
Another beat, lower, almost annoyed at herself.
“So I don’t know why your ‘I won’t say it anymore’ sounds like someone turning off a light I was still using.” Her gloved fingers tightened around the untouched mug. “Don’t look at me. I know I’m the one who made the case for caution. I just… didn’t expect the caution to arrive so quickly. Or to feel like loss when it did.”
The empty line of her neck inclined away from him a fraction, then back again.
“…Forget I said that. You did the right thing.”
The smoke curling above her collar made a liar of the sentence.
Curtis blinked at her, mismatched eyes narrowing as the contradiction finished arranging itself in real time. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt.
“…Um. What?!” A short, helpless beat. “You’re impossible, Celty.”
Then he started laughing.
Not sharp. Not mocking. The low, disbelieving kind of laugh that belonged to a man who had tried to do the careful thing for once and immediately been punished by the same person who requested the caution. He tipped his head back against the couch cushion, one hand briefly covering his face as the absurdity of it ran through him.
Celty’s black smoke spiked hard above her collar.
Her real voice snapped through the private channel, flustered and defensive at once.
“Do not laugh at me.”
A short pause. The smoke tangled on itself.
“I am allowed to warn you about a door and still dislike the sound of it closing. That is not impossible. That is—” she faltered, then pushed on more weakly, “—complicated.”
Another beat, lower, mortified by how thin the defense sounded even to herself.
“…Stop laughing. You understood the risk, you withdrew the words, and now you’re sitting there amused because I didn’t enjoy the success of my own argument. That is extremely rude.” The empty line of her neck turned toward him despite the complaint. “And if you say ‘impossible’ one more time, I am going back to the roof without you.”
The black smoke drifting across his sleeve made the threat look a lot more like someone who was still, inconveniently, staying put.
Curtis kept laughing a second longer, then dragged a breath in and tried to speak through what was left of it, long black braids shifting as he turned toward her more fully.
“No — don’t go, Celty.” His voice was still warm with amusement, but the point underneath it came clean. “It’s just… what do you want from me? Or want me to say? I can just be me, but that means I’ll say things that come to my mind and heart. You wanted an honest version — that’s the most complete — but you want to revoke some of the things I just do.”
The laughter thinned into something steadier. Not unkind. Just exact.
Celty went still on the couch beside him. Black smoke above her collar rose hard, then stalled, as if even the shadow had been forced to stop and examine itself. For a moment the private channel held only the discomfort of a woman being handed her own contradiction with both hands and no soft padding.
When her real voice finally came through, it was quieter and far less defended than the threat to leave the room.
“…That is a fair question.”
A short pause.
“I do want the honest version. I asked for it. I stayed for it. I told you not to manage the distance, not to feed me only the safe pieces, not to talk to the walls while I was sitting right here.” The smoke drifted unevenly between them. “And then the honest version said I was beautiful, said it without flinching, and I felt the ground under my life move — so I asked you to stop. That is inconsistent. I know it is.”
Another heavier beat.
“What I want from you may be unreasonable. I want you to keep being whole in front of me. I also want not to be changed by it faster than I can afford. Those two desires are already in conflict.” Her gloved fingers tightened around the untouched mug. “So when you ask what I want you to say… the true answer is that I want your unedited mind and heart, and I am also afraid of the sentences that come with them.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him, less certain than before.
“I don’t get to demand complete honesty and then run a revision board over the parts that land too close. You’re right about that.” A thinner curl of smoke. “Just… give me a second when the true version of you rearranges more than I expected. That is all I can ask for without becoming the exact kind of dishonest I said I didn’t want.”
Elsewhere, the apartment smelled like a night that had not bothered with subtlety.
Shinra and Mei sat among scattered notes and half-cleared desk space, the academic pretense of “looking something over” only thinly covering the residual disorder of the evening before. Shinra, glasses low on his nose, thumbed out a casual text to Celty with the same bright absentmindedness he used for grocery lists and medical observations.
Mei wrinkled her nose, pink hair falling forward as she glanced around the room.
“We should mop up or spray something. It smells like—well. It smells like you had every part of me last night.”
Shinra did not look up from his phone.
“Celty can’t smell. It’s fine.”
Mei stared at him for a full second.
Then she let out a short, incredulous breath and leaned back in the chair, arms folding.
“…That’s your standard? Not ‘we should clean because it’s basic respect,’ not ‘maybe don’t leave evidence of an affair in the communal air,’ just she can’t smell, therefore fine?” Her mouth curled, half amused and half appalled. “Shinra, that is an impressively efficient kind of ugliness. Very scientific. Very convenient.”
She tipped her head, eyes narrowing with interest more than moral shock.
“You’re lucky I’m not the type to get sentimental about being treated like an untraceable variable. Still. If your headless woman ever does come home early, I hope your notes are more persuasive than your housekeeping.”
A beat, dryer.
“Text her all you want. Just don’t insult both of us by calling this ‘fine’ like the only problem in the room is olfactory.”
Shinra finally looked up from the phone, glasses catching the light as he offered Mei the same mild, untroubled smile he used when a diagnosis was inconvenient but not, in his mind, damning.
“You’re assigning moral architecture to a practical observation,” he said lightly. “Celty perceives the world differently from us. Shadow, vibration, presence, pattern. Not the same chemical map a human nose depends on. So no, the residual… evidence is unlikely to register to her the way it would to you or me. That makes it fine in the immediate sense.”
He set the phone face-down beside the notes and adjusted his sleeves with neat, absent care.
“And I do respect her. Profoundly. What exists between Celty and me is not erased by a night of simpler physical reciprocity with someone who understands the temporary terms.” His smile thinned, almost gentle. “You and I are not building a counter-home. We’re acknowledging a pressure release. That doesn’t require incense and confessionals.”
A short pause. The brightness in his face did not fully hide the rationalization under it.
“If she came home early, I would handle it. I always do. Celty is not fragile in the way people assume, and our bond has survived far more structural strain than an unclean room.” He glanced at Mei again, fond and faintly detached at once. “You can call the efficiency ugly if you like. I call it knowing which variables actually threaten the system… and which ones only offend ordinary sentiment.”
He picked the phone back up, as if the matter had been adequately filed.
“Besides. She trusts me to come home to her. That has always been the more important fact.”
The quiet in Curtis’s flat broke in two directions at once.
Celty’s PDA vibrated first.
She glanced down, black smoke rising in a sudden, tighter ribbon from the empty space above her collar as Shinra’s name lit the screen. A short, ordinary message — the kind that assumed access, continuity, and the right to reach her without friction:
Shinra:
Celty~ Are you still out? I made tea earlier and then got distracted with notes. Coming home soon?
The words were light. Familiar. Completely unaware of the couch she was sitting on, the untouched coffee in her gloved hand, or the man whose morning she had already let rearrange her.
Before she could answer, Curtis’s phone started ringing.
Anri’s name showed on the screen.
Curtis looked at it, then at Celty, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as the canceled school day suddenly acquired more than one claim on the room. He answered after a beat, voice carefully leveled back toward normal.
“…Anri?”
Her voice came through softer than Rio’s had been, careful and a little hesitant.
“Curtis-kun. I saw that classes were canceled. I… wanted to make sure you were all right. After everything.” A short pause. “Are you free today?”
On the couch, two separate lives entered the same air without knowing they were sharing it.
Celty sat motionless with Shinra’s text still open, black smoke curling hard above her collar.
Curtis held the phone to his ear, mismatched eyes lowered, while Anri’s concern reached for him from a quieter, more fragile distance.
The morning’s agreement — honesty without a conclusion — suddenly had company neither of them had invited.
Curtis did not answer Anri right away.
Celty did not answer Shinra either.
For a stretched second they only looked at each other across the small distance of the couch — his phone still raised to his ear, her PDA still lit with an ordinary message from a life that expected her back. Long black braids rested against the black compression shirt. Black smoke rose in a thin, unsettled ribbon from the empty space above Celty’s collar. Neither of them needed the private channel to feel the same realization settle into the room.
Rio had already called.
Anri was on the line now.
Shinra was asking when she was coming home.
And in the middle of all that ordinary reaching sat the fact of last night, the roof, the held hand, the honesty without a conclusion. What had begun as coincidence, then convenience, then confession was no longer contained by any of those words. It had become a point around which other lives were already starting to turn, scrape, and collide.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes held the empty space above her collar.
Celty faced him without the helmet, without the protection of distance, and did not look away.
This was no longer just a complicated morning.
It was the beginning of something complicated enough to have consequences.
Outside, Ikebukuro continued under a canceled school day, unaware that two of its more impossible people had just recognized the same truth in the same quiet room:
Whatever they chose next, someone was going to feel it.
Neither of them looked away.
The phone at Curtis’s ear and the PDA in Celty’s gloved hand remained raised like unfinished obligations, but the center of the room had already moved elsewhere — into the stretch of air between them, into the long look that held more want than either had formally agreed to allow. Long black braids rested against the black compression shirt. Black smoke rose in a slow, unguarded ribbon from the empty space above Celty’s collar. For a moment the canceled school day, the city, and every waiting name outside the flat fell back to second place.
They were thinking the same thing.
Blow it off.
Leave the messages unanswered.
Let Anri’s concern go to voicemail.
Let Shinra’s tea and notes and assumed return wait.
Stay in the apartment with the cold coffee and the disordered bed and the first version of honesty that had not immediately demanded a conclusion. Spend the whole empty day inside the temporary shelter of each other’s presence before the world could start collecting debts again.
It would have been easy in the narrow sense.
The door was closed. The morning was already compromised. No class required them. No emergency had yet knocked.
But both of them knew better.
Curtis had lives reaching for him now whether he wanted the weight or not — Rio’s soft claim, Anri’s careful concern, the van gang’s unfinished reading of him, a city that had started placing his name. Celty had a home that still expected her, a man who believed continuity was the same thing as understanding, and a role in Ikebukuro that did not pause because her chest had become complicated overnight.
Wanting the day was simple.
Taking it would make the complication irreversible faster than either of them could pretend to handle.
So they only stared, longing held in check by the knowledge of the lives already attached to them, while the phone kept waiting at his ear and the unanswered text kept glowing in her hand.
Celty moved first.
Her gloved thumb crossed the PDA screen with deliberate finality.
Celty:
I’ll be home soon.
The message sent. Across the city, Shinra would receive it as continuity restored. In the flat, it landed more like a door being set back on its hinges while everyone could still see the frame had changed.
Curtis exhaled once and lifted the phone fully back to his ear, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt.
“…Yeah, Anri. I’m free for a bit. We can hang out.” A short pause as she answered, relief audible even from Celty’s side of the couch. “I’ll meet you. Text me the place.”
He ended the call.
For a second the apartment held only the aftermath of two responsible choices. Then Celty reached over and set her gloved hand on his — not restraining, not dramatic, just present against the back of his hand where the warmth of the decision still lingered.
Her real voice came through the private channel low and steady.
“…Look at us. Doing the correct thing.”
A short pause. The black smoke above her collar drifted toward him in a thinner, more reluctant ribbon.
“I told him I’ll be home soon. You told her you’d meet her. That is what people with lives do.” Her fingers tightened once over his. “I need you to know I didn’t want the correct thing first. I wanted the version where we ignored both messages and let the day stay closed around this room.”
Another quieter beat.
“Since we didn’t choose that… at least let this be true before I put the helmet on and you walk out into the city. I am still here with you in the part that wanted to stay.”
The empty line of her neck inclined toward him.
“Don’t make the correct thing mean the other thing never happened.”
Curtis turned his hand under hers until their fingers were properly joined, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as he met the empty space above her collar without flinching.
“This all happened. I’m never going to deny you that.” A short pause. His voice dropped, steadier. “Come back if you don’t feel like being at home… I know you said don’t bother that door. But this time, I am.”
The invitation sat in the quiet flat with no disguise on it. Not a hypothetical. Not a softer version of distance. A deliberate reopening of the exact threshold she had warned him about.
Celty’s black smoke rose hard, then slowly settled as the private channel filled with the weight of a choice she was no longer pretending not to recognize. When her real voice came through, it was low and stripped of every earlier defense.
“…Alright.”
A short pause.
“I’ll come back. If being home starts feeling like a room I’m only performing continuity inside of… I’ll come here.” Her gloved fingers tightened around his. “You did bother the door. And I’m walking through the answer instead of pretending I didn’t hear it.”
Another quieter beat, almost resigned to her own honesty.
“Don’t make me regret saying yes out loud. And don’t act surprised when I actually use it.” The empty line of her neck inclined toward him. “For now… I have to go. Put the helmet on. Become the version of me the rest of the city expects. But this morning stays. And so does the invitation.”
Her hand remained in his one second longer before she finally began to let go.
“I’ll see you, Curtis.”
Before she could fully step back, Curtis pulled her in.
The hug was sudden and complete — both arms around her, long black braids falling forward over the black compression shirt as he held the headless woman against his chest with none of the morning’s earlier awkwardness left in the motion. For a second the flat narrowed to fabric, warmth, and the quiet pressure of a decision neither of them was taking back.
“Don’t make me regret this, Celty.” His voice sat low against the place where her ear would have been. “I’ll see you later.”
Celty stiffened only for half a breath, then folded into it. Black smoke rose in a soft, uncontrolled ribbon from the empty space above her collar as her gloved hands came up and held him in return, gripping the back of his shirt like the correct choices waiting outside the door could be postponed for one more human second.
Her real voice came through the private channel quiet and close.
“…I won’t.”
A short pause.
“Don’t you make me regret it either.” Her arms tightened once. “Go meet Anri. I’ll go home. We’ll both do the responsible thing with the part of the day that still belongs to other people.” Another softer beat. “And when the responsible thing starts feeling like a lie… I’ll come back. You opened the door. I’m not going to pretend it closed just because we have to walk out of it right now.”
She stayed in the hug a moment longer, smoke drifting against his shoulder, then slowly eased back enough to recover the shape of leaving.
“…Later, Curtis.”